<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100801 (level 7). Domain: soc_selfcheck,wazuh,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="soc_selfcheck,wazuh,">

  <!-- auditd NOT RUNNING or NOT INSTALLED: the heartbeat reports an EMPTY
       audit_enabled value (`audit_enabled=` with no number, e.g.
       `audit_enabled= audit_rules=0`). auditctl is unreachable - the kernel
       audit subsystem is absent, not switched off by someone.

       This is a MONITORING BLIND SPOT, not confirmed tampering. It fires once
       per heartbeat (every 5 min) on every host that never had auditd, so at
       level 13 it flooded: 982 alerts/day across production hosts with no
       auditd (nextcloud, wireguard, bookstack), and one of those false alarms
       drove an autonomous host isolation of a production box (purple-team,
       2026-07-31). MEDIUM (level 7) puts it below MIN_LEVEL (12): the soc-agent
       no longer opens an incident from it, so it can no longer trigger an
       autonomous action - it stays an operational signal ("install/start
       auditd here") without masquerading as an attack.

       The GENUINE runtime disable (`audit_enabled=0`, someone turned it off) is
       a different, hostile case: rule 100807 (heartbeat) and rule 100805
       (CONFIG_CHANGE, immediate + auid) carry it at HIGH. The regexes are
       mutually exclusive on the value (empty here, `0` there), so load order
       does not arbitrate between them. -->

  <rule id="100801" level="7">
    <if_sid>100800</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">audit_enabled=(?:\s|$)</match>
    <description>auditd not running or not installed - kernel audit status unavailable (monitoring blind spot, not confirmed tampering)</description>
  </rule>

</group>
